it's great to welcome to the program  today John Barry who is author of the  book the great influenza the story of  the deadliest pandemic in history also  professor at Tulane University's School  of Public Health and Tropical Medicine  John so great to have you on thanks so  we've heard a number of different  assertions made about the flu pandemic  of 1918 from the President and from  others sort of in the context of  coronavirus let's just figure out a  little bit more about that what are the  origins of that pandemic well no one  knows for certain like Koba 19 and  animal virus pollen torrents of viruses  have their natural reservoir and birds  but they can infect mammals an animal  virus jump species to humans spread  around the world  infected about the third of the world's  population and killed 50 to 100 million  people adjusted for population that  would equal about 220 to 440 million  people today so fortunately as bad as  kovin 19 is it's not nearly as lethal as  a 1918 pandemic what was the sort of  full duration as we understand it of  that pandemic well it really in total  covered from the spring of 1918 until  the fall of 1920  however 2/3 of the deaths perhaps more  than two-thirds of the deaths occurred  in a 14 or 15 week period in the fall of  1918 the first wave was very spotty miss  we're not really sure exactly but my  guess is it although it was pretty  widespread in Western Europe and in u.s.  army camps in the spring it probably  missed most of this country and most of  the world then a I think the virus mean  was also very mild so mild  there were medical journal articles that  said this looks and smells like  influenza but it's not killing enough  people so it's probably not influencing  that turned around very rapidly the  virus mutated and then you started  seeing scientists saying this looks and  smells like influenza but we've never  seen in influenza that killed like this  although it was of course in that second  way as I said 14 to 15 weeks from mid  September through most of December then  there was a third wave in late winter  and in early spring of 1919 so it's very  unusual to have two waves of influenza  in the same influenza season very  unusual so the virus had mutated again I  guess then in 1920 it came back and that  was pretty much it the virus probably  mutated further toward mildness and in  addition our immune systems having seen  it several times began to deal with it  more efficiently so it gradually became  seasonal influenza do you know the  president often refers to 1917 when  talking about that pandemic I have to  ask him okay you there's no there's  nothing been your understanding I have  history  nothing scientifically or  epidemiologically that would justify  calling at nineteen seventeen you know  my guess that he said it once and he  doesn't like to admit any error over  even the tiniest things  so rather than correct himself in future  he just kind of wrapped his arms around  it as he seems to do with other mistakes  fair in terms of the name often called  the Spanish flu can you talk about  number one why it has that name but also  sort of the early hot spots that we  and think about as the places where it  really started to spread I most likely  explanation for the name is that Spain  was not at war the warring countries  censored the press I didn't like to  print anything that was negative that  might hurt morale that might hurt the  war effort  Spain wasn't at war plus the king got  sick celebrity culture then is now so  the Spanish press wrote about it and  other newspapers around the world wrote  about it and picked up the name Spanish  Influenza except in Spain where they  called it other things  the first known eruption was actually in  January in rural Kansas of southwest  corner of the state and in early  February we see it in New York City you  know it hit the army camps in first and  camp Funston in Kansas Ford Riley in  early March spread to about half of the  army camps in the United States during  the spring seemed to travel with the US  troops to Europe although would have  gotten there anyway as I said it was in  New York and almost certainly other  places even before the army camp so it  would have spread eventually but the US  troops did seem to bring it to Europe in  late March and April they accelerated  the spread and you know it  as I said earlier widespread in Western  Europe  Ludendorff the German commander launched  an offensive to try to win the war  before American soldiers arrived in  large numbers he blamed an outbreak of  influenza among his troops for the  failure of that offensive of course  I think that's looking for an excuse  there  influence on the other side of the  trenches as well although the first  known cases were in January in rural  Kansas the scientific community thinks  that the and it is possible that it  actually started there but and I  advanced that hypothesis in my book but  there's been a lot of work since my book  came out originally in 2004 and I myself  had backed off the Kansas hypothesis I  think it's more likely it started in  China other other hypotheses on some  very good virologists think it started  in France Viet Nam's the possibility and  you never know it could have been New  York City most virologists think that it  was circulating for at least a year  perhaps learning how to better infect  humans and in some think it was  circulating for a couple years so again  we'll never know where it actually did  start that spring waving was spotty and  mild in July the first widespread  outbreaks of the lethal second wave were  noted in Switzerland in fact it was it  was so bad in Switzerland US military  intelligence report said that this is  being called influenza but it's actually  the Black Plague of the Middle Ages hmm  wasn't it was influenza when it when it  comes to the obviously Medicine was very  different at that time international  travel was different at that time media  was different at that time when it comes  to mitigation what was it that was done  you know we talk about the quarantines  and the shutdowns are unprecedented and  in a sense they are but there were also  some of these techniques to probably  back then  exactly most cities in the United States  not quite all of them but very close to  all of them closed all sorts of things  they weren't actually quite as Extreme  as what we've done they closed places  public gathering schools etc most of  them did it too late to have maximum  impact just as I think in the United  States we close down too late to have  maximum impact as we're speaking there  was a study from Columbia University  that came out today that said if we  close down a week earlier we would have  saved more than one third of the lives  that of law have been lost so far and if  we close down two weeks earlier we would  have saved about 60% of the lives that  have been lost so far similar things  happened in 1918 most cities closed down  too late to have maximum impact when we  think about the communication that we  now see of federal and state guidelines  here augmented by television radio  internet social media etc how was it in  1918 through 1920 that most people were  learning about what was supposed to be  done  to stop the spread well of course print  was the only media you didn't even have  radio yet you had rumor and you had  print we were in a different situation  than because of the war the government  the Wilson administration focused  everything on winning the war they  passed a law which made it punishable by  20 years in prison  to quote Otto write print or publish any  disloyal profane scurrilous or abusive  language about the form of government in  the United States so you could curse  some action the government took and you  just broke the law truth was not a  defense this law was prosecuted  vigorously  congressman was sentenced to more than  10 years in prison for this law it's  also the one that a couple of memorable  phrases came out of the Supreme Court  decision which actually upheld it Oliver  Wendell Holmes who is generally  considered a signal you know real  upholder of freedom of speech actually  wrote the opinion upholding this law and  he used a phrase clear and present  danger in his opinion along with the  analogy you can't shout fire in a  crowded theater to justify upholding  this law anyway that was important  because and also the government had  created a propaganda arm and the  architect of that what was called the  committee for Public Information I wrote  that  nothing an experience teaches us that  truth is superior to falsehood the value  of an idea lies in its inspirational  value it matters very little if it is  true or false so on the one hand the  government was lying to people in order  to keep morale up for the war on the  other hand they were threatening people  with 20 years in the can for telling the  truth if the truth seems critical of the  government so influenza fell into that  infrastructure National Public Health  leaders associated with the government  said things like this is ordinary  influenza by another name or you have  nothing to fear if proper precautions  are taken neither of those statements  were true but they were echoed in most  places around the country many you know  not just echo but quoted verbatim in in  most studies so initially people  we're led to believe this was ordinary  influenza by another name but it didn't  take long  we're talking about almost a matter of  days before they realized this was  something very different I wish we had  more time to dig into it further but I  do want to make sure people know the  book is the great influenza the story of  the deadliest pandemic in history and  we've been speaking with the book's  author John Berry from Tulane University  John I so appreciate your time you're  very welcome

